  - [1mAllowlist for remote, full detail in console[0m — the logger takes structured fields; only keys on an explicit
    allowlist (level, message, timestamp, event name, error type, duration, a generated session id) are serialized to
    the sink. Everything else still prints locally. Buys you a guarantee that survives future code: a developer who logs
    a whole form object cannot leak it remotely, because the allowlist drops unknown keys by default. Costs friction —
    adding a new field to production logs means adding it to the allowlist, and you'll hit that a few times. Rules out
    ad-hoc "just log the object and look at it later."
  - [1mDenylist redaction[0m — ship whatever is passed, but scrub keys matching [38;5;153mpassword[39m/[38;5;153mtoken[39m/[38;5;153msecret[39m/[38;5;153mauth[39m/[38;5;153mcookie[39m and mask
    their values. Buys convenience: log what you want, common credentials are caught. Costs correctness — it only
    catches patterns you anticipated. A field named [38;5;153mpw[39m, [38;5;153mcredentials[39m, or a password nested inside a form object passed as
    one value gets through. This is the industry-common choice and also the industry-common source of credential leaks.
  - [1mNo policy[0m — transmit what's logged; treat "don't log secrets" as a review rule. Cheapest now, and it makes the leak
    a matter of when.

  [1mMy recommendation: the allowlist[0m, specifically because this is a login form. The failure mode of the denylist isn't
  hypothetical here — [38;5;153mvalidateForm[39m already receives [38;5;153m{username, password}[39m as one object, and logging that object during a
  debugging session is the natural thing someone does at 2am. The allowlist makes that inert. The friction is real but
  it's a one-line change each time, and the console still shows everything locally.

  Separately, and regardless of which you pick: I will not log the password value under any option, and I'd drop the
  existing username from the [3mremote[0m payload in favor of a session id unless you want usernames queryable in your logs —
  tell me if you do, since that's a genuine debugging convenience with a genuine privacy cost.
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
[39m←  ☒ Redaction [38;5;16m[48;5;153m ☐ Usernames [39m[49m ✔ Submit  →

[1m[38;5;231mShould usernames be queryable in shipped logs?[0m

[38;5;153m❯[39m [38;5;246m1.[39m [38;5;153mNo, session id only (Recommended)[39m
     [38;5;246mRemote records carry a generated per-session id instead of the username. You can still correlate a user's events; [39m
     [38;5;246mthe collector never stores the identifier itself.[39m
  [38;5;246m2.[39m Yes, include usernames
     [38;5;246mMakes "what happened to this specific user" directly searchable, which is a real support convenience. Puts account
[39m     [38;5;246midentifiers in a third-party system and widens the privacy and breach surface.[39m
  [38;5;246m3.[39m Hashed username
     [38;5;246mA stable one-way hash: the same user correlates across sessions, but the raw identifier is not stored. Reversible [39m
     [38;5;246mby brute force for common usernames, so treat it as pseudonymous, not anonymous.[39m
  [38;5;246m4. Type something.[39m
[38;5;246m────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
